# NPS-SLAUGHTER-PEN: Kathleen R. Georg, "Our Principal Loss Was in This Place": Action at the Slaughter Pen and at the South End of Houck's Ridge, 2 July 1863, Gettysburg National Military Park, 18 March 1984 (a photocopied typescript of 60 pages; the pages are read from the page images and aligned to the OCR text made by scripts/ocr.py).
# The study is a park historian's synthesis of 1984. It cites no source for its statements about the land (it says 'apparently' where it infers); veterans' recollections it quotes were written 1886-1901 and are marked hedged. A statement about the vegetation of 1984 or about a later feature is use indirect or none.
# This second file continues a4-slaughter-pen.xdef (PDF pp. 7-9, read from the damaged text layer). Pages viewed as images on 2026-11-16 are listed in %images.
@ NPS-SLAUGHTER-PEN
%images 2, 3, 4, 5, 12, 14, 15, 16, 18

2 | general | The Round Tops and the Slaughter Pen were photographed in July 1863 by O'Sullivan, Gibson, Gardner and Brady; the study says Devil's Den was not | - | 1863-07 (the photographs of July 1863; the study names the photographers and gives no plate numbers on this page) | stated | The study says the Round Tops and the Slaughter Pen were photographed by the early chroniclers of the battlefield, O'Sullivan, Gibson, Gardner and Brady, before the month of July 1863 passed, and that Devil's Den was not | =The Round Tops and Slaughter Pen were photographed by those early chroniclers of the battlefield before the month of July 1863 dissipated- -O'Sullivan, Gibson, Gardner, and Brady. Devil's {{Nen|Den}} was not. ~O
2 | woodlots | By 1984 foliage had occluded the Slaughter Pen as part of the natural environment of Big Round Top | - | ~1984 (the park historian's account of foliage that grew after the battle; she gives no date for its start) | stated | The study says the Slaughter Pen slipped into virtual obscurity, aided by the annual growth of foliage that occluded it as little more than part of the natural environment of Big Round Top | =While Little Round Top retained its prominence because of the very nature of its visibly tactical importance, the Slaughter Pen slipped into virtual obsurity, aided by annual growth of foliage which occluded it as little more than part of Big Round Top's natural environment. ~O
3 | woodlots | The strewn boulders of Plum Run and the Slaughter Pen to be cleared of vegetation and made visible as in 1863 | - | 1863-07 (the study's statement of the 1863 condition, inside a recommendation of 1984; it gives no source) | hedged | The study says the strewn boulders of Plum Run and the Slaughter Pen should be cleared of vegetation and made visible to the visitor as in 1863, when the site became a vast graveyard for Confederates from Alabama and Georgia | =The strewn boulders of Plum Run and the Slaughter Pen should be cleared of vegetation and made visible to the visitor as in 1863, when the site became a vast graveyard for Confederates from Alabama and Georgia, cut down by artillery and musketry fire from the Valley of Death. ~O
3 | later | The old electric trolley bed ran through the Slaughter Pen and below Houck's Ridge; a Devil's Den by-pass was proposed along it | - | !later than 1863 (the trolley bed of the electric railway; the by-pass is a proposal of the 1980s) | stated | The study says the general management plan recommended removing a portion of Sickles Avenue and building a Devil's Den by-pass along the route of the old electric trolley bed through the Slaughter Pen and below Houck's Ridge | =The General Management Plan for Gettysburg National Military Park recommended the obliteration of a portion of Sickles Avenue and the construction of a Devil's Den by-pass, following the route of the old electric trolley bed through Slaughter Pen and below Houck's Ridge. ~O
4 | general | Local names of the Devil's Den rocks in the early nineteenth century: The Big Rocks and Raccoon Den (Emmanuel Bushman's recollection) | - | pre-1863 (early decades of the nineteenth century, as an early resident remembered them in 1884) | hedged | The study says an early local resident remembered the folks of the immediate farming community calling the place The Big Rocks and Raccoon Den in the early decades of the nineteenth century, citing Emmanuel Bushman in the Gettysburg Compiler | =He remembered the folks of the immediate farming community calling it "The Big Rocks" and "Raccoon Den" in the early decades of the nineteenth century. ~O | =Before the two armies marched to Gettysburg from their different compass headings there was a {{Nevil's|Devil's}} Den. Although an early {{Tocal|local}} resident never recollected hearing the name in his youth, he had heard stories about the place.
4 | general | The name Devil's Den in local use since at least the 1840s | - | pre-1863 (the study says 'apparently'; the name was published by Michael Jacobs shortly after the battle) | hedged | The study says others of the area had apparently called the place Devil's Den since at least the 1840s and that Michael Jacobs perpetuated the name in publication shortly after the battle | =But others of the area had apparently called it Devil's Den since at least the 1840s, and the name was perpetuated in publication by Michael Jacobs shortly after the battle. ~O
4 | general | Bachelder: the name Devil's Den was given to the locality before the battle | - | pre-1863 (Bachelder's statement in testimony of 1894, as the study quotes it) | stated | The study quotes Bachelder's testimony of the October Session of 1894 that the Devil's Den is not a new name and was a name given to the locality before the battle | =The Devil's Den is not a new name; it was a name given to the locality before the battle. ~O | @5: =October Session, 1894, pp. 289-290.
4 | general | Bachelder: the Devil's Den is a hole under ledges with a spring, its front masked by a big boulder | - | ~1894 (Bachelder's description in testimony of 1894; a statement about the place as he knew it, not a survey of 1863) | stated | The study quotes Bachelder's testimony of the October Session of 1894 that the Devil's Den is a hole under ledges in which there is a spring and that its front is masked by a big boulder so that it cannot be seen until one is within ten feet | =The Devil's Den is a hole under ledges in which there is a spring. The front of it is masked by a big bowlder, and you cannot see it {{unt7Tl|until}} you get within ten feet of it. ~O | @5: =October Session, 1894, pp. 289-290.
5 | general | The spring at Devil's Den appears in a Tipton stereo view but no longer flows, filled in or altered by avenue construction | - | ~after 1863 (a Tipton stereo view whose date the page does not give; the spring altered by the construction of the avenue, a later feature) | stated | The study says the spring Bachelder mentioned appears in the stereo photograph of the Tipton view The Spring at Devil's Den but has either been filled in or altered by avenue construction and no longer flows | =The spring mentioned by Bachelder appears in the above stereo photograph, but it has either been filled in or altered by avenue construction as it no longer flows. ~O | =Tipton Stereo View. "The Spring at Devil's Den"
5 | woodlots | In 1863 the woods of Big Round Top were natural in growth, the owner having at one time publicly forbidden by fine any cutting of wood on his portion of the summit; the other sites nearby showed human use | - | 1863 (the condition 'at that time in 1863'; the study gives no source and no date for the owner's prohibition) | stated | The study says the other sites and areas in the vicinity showed the effects of human use and development in 1863 with the exception of Big Round Top, whose woods were natural in growth, the owner at one time publicly forbidding by fine any cutting of wood on his portion of the summit | =The other sites and areas in the vicinity showed the effects of human use and development at that time in 1863 with the exception of Big Round Top. The woods of Big Round Top were natural in growth, the owner at one time publicly forbidding by fine any cutting of wood on his portion of the summit. ~O
12 | woodlots | The right of the 124th New York extended just into Rose Woods behind Smith's battery before the attack | bld-rose-farm | 1863-07-02 | stated | The study says the 124th New York, with its right extending just into Rose Woods behind Smith, spent its time before the battle opened conserving its energies | =The 124th New York, with its right extending just into Rose Woods behind Smith, spent its time before the battle opened conserving its energies for the fight: ~O
12 | fields | Pasture at the lower end of the Wheatfield; the G. W. Weikert (Timbers) farm in front of the 4th Maine; the Houck's Ridge area used as pasture | - | 1863-07-02 (hedged by the study: the heifer 'may have come' from one of three places) | hedged | The study says the heifer eaten or killed near the 4th Maine may have come from the pasture at the lower end of the Wheatfield, the G. W. Weikert (Timbers) farm in front of the regiment or the Houck's Ridge area itself, which was used as pasture | =The heifer may {{haye|have}} come from one of three places in the {{vicinity --the|vicinity---the}} pasture at the lower end of the Wheatfield to their right, the G. W. Weikert (Timbers) Farm to their front, or perhaps from the Houck's Ridge area itself, which was used as pasture. ~O
14 | woodlots | Law's pioneers felled the timber in front of the 4th Alabama on the afternoon of July 2; a Confederate battery then occupied the newly cleared space | - | 1863-07-02 (the study's narrative of Captain Ward's recollection of 1900; 'probably' for the battery) | hedged | The study says the pioneer corps of Law's brigade passed to the front of the 4th Alabama and felled the timber there with axes, that the work drew Smith's fire, and that a battery of Henry's battalion, probably Reilly's Rowan Artillery, occupied the newly cleared space and returned fire | =Immediately upon getting into position the pioneer corps of Law's Brigade passed to the front of the 4th {{Alabma|Alabama}} and proceeded to fell the timber there with their axes. The {{actiyity|activity}} drew Smith's fire, which killed several men in the 5th Texas, just to the left of Law's Brigade. However, one of the batteries of Henry's Battalion, probably Reilly's Rowan Artillery, immediately occupied the newly cleared space and returned fire. ~O
14 | woodlots | The 124th New York moved by the right flank into the woods on which its right rested, and judged them more hazardous than the old position | bld-rose-farm | 1863-07-02 (a veteran's recollection printed in the National Tribune; hedged) | hedged | The study quotes a veteran of the 124th New York who wrote that the colonel moved the regiment by the right flank into the woods on which its right rested and that the woods made the new position more hazardous than the one just abandoned | ={{Qur|Our}} Colonel (Ellis) moved us by the right-flank into the woods on which our right rested, "I judge he thought after he had got us in there that instead of the woods being a protection they made our new position more hazardous than the one we had just abandoned. ~O | =[A. W. Tucker, "Orange Blossoms," {{Nationa? Tribune.(21|National Tribune. (21}} January 1886).]
15 | fences | A long line of Federal skirmishers protected by a stone wall opened fire on the 4th Alabama as it advanced | - | 1863-07-02 (a member of the regiment, in an account the study dates to 1900; hedged) | hedged | The study quotes a member of the 4th Alabama who wrote that a long line of Federal skirmishers, protected by a stone wall, immediately opened fire as the regiment advanced | =A long line of Federal {{skiymtshers,|skirmishers,}} protected by a stone wall, immediately opened fire. ~O | =According to a member of the regiment:
16 | fences | Billy Marshall and another soldier ran to be first at the stone fence behind which the Federal skirmishers lay | - | 1863-07-02 (the same veteran account, cited as Ward; hedged) | hedged | The study quotes the veteran account that two soldiers ran neck and neck, each striving to be first at the stone fence behind which lay protected the Federal line of skirmishers | =each striving to be the first at the stone fence, behind which lay protected the Federal line of skirmishers, firing into the faces of the advancing Confederates. ~O | =firing into the faces of the advancing Confederates." {{IWard,|[Ward,}} p. 347.]
16 | buildings | The 4th Alabama tore down the slopes from the ridge towards the Bushman and Slyder farm buildings | bld-bushman-farm,bld-slyder-farm | 1863-07-02 | stated | The study says the 4th Alabamians were tearing down the slopes from the ridge towards the Bushman and Slyder farm buildings | =Yet Adjutant Terrell pointed out how orderly the Texans were marching in comparison to Scrugg's 4th Alabamians, who were tearing down the slopes from the ridge towards the Bushman and Slyder Farm buildings. ~O
18 | fields | Law and Robertson advanced from Bushman Ridge through the plowed and farmed fields of the Bushman and Slyder farms | bld-bushman-farm,bld-slyder-farm | 1863-07-02 | stated | The study says Law and Robertson advanced from Bushman Ridge through the plowed and farmed fields of the Bushman and Slyder farms and made easy targets for Smith's battery and for the skirmishers at the stone wall in the valley | =As Law and Robertson advanced from Bushman Ridge, through the plowed and farmed fields of the Bushman and {{STtyder|Slyder}} Farms, they made easy targets for the four rifled guns of Smith's Battery and for the sharpshooters and skirmishers at the stone wall in the valley. ~O
18 | fences | Sharpshooters and skirmishers held a stone wall in the valley below Houck's Ridge | - | 1863-07-02 | stated | The study says the sharpshooters and skirmishers were at the stone wall in the valley when Law and Robertson advanced | =As Law and Robertson advanced from Bushman Ridge, through the plowed and farmed fields of the Bushman and {{STtyder|Slyder}} Farms, they made easy targets for the four rifled guns of Smith's Battery and for the sharpshooters and skirmishers at the stone wall in the valley. ~O
18 | woodlots | Confederate skirmishers emerged from Bushman Woods, followed by the heavy lines of infantry | bld-bushman-farm | 1863-07-02 (the study's narrative of the recollection in Maine at Gettysburg) | hedged | The study says the first hint of Hood's division was the clouds of Confederate skirmishers emerging from Bushman Woods, followed by the heavy lines of infantry | =the first hint of whom was the "clouds of Confederate skirmishers" emerging from Bushman Woods, followed by the heavy lines of infantry. ~O
18 | woodlots | Benning's and Anderson's massed columns emerged from Bushman Woods in support | bld-bushman-farm | 1863-07-02 (General Ward's recollection as the study gives it; hedged) | hedged | The study says General Ward would remember the massed columns of Benning and Anderson in support emerging from Bushman Woods | =General Ward would remember the Confederate skirmishers, the first heavy line of infantry of Law and Robertson marching to oppose his line, and then the massed columns of Benning and Anderson in support emerging from Bushman Woods. ~O
